@packages/core/src/model/diagnostics.sigil from SigilDiagnosticModel import { DiagnosticModel }

component SigilImplementationOwnership {
  goal {
    Provide the shared core query that returns implementation targets linked to a
    Sigil contract from implementation comments.

    Keep implementation-to-contract ownership discovery deterministic and reusable
    for CLI and LSP without writing annotations into Sigil source.
  }

  interface {
    ImplementationEvidenceInput {
      contains exactly workspaceSnapshotIdentity, discoveryState, sources, and
      diagnostics

      workspaceSnapshotIdentity is copied from the ResolvedSigilWorkspace for which
      the host performed implementation-source discovery

      discoveryState is exactly complete or unavailable

      sources is an immutable ordered collection of ImplementationSourceRecord values

      diagnostics is an immutable ordered collection of complete SigilDiagnostic
      records from DiagnosticModel, owned by SigilDiagnosticModel

      ImplementationSourceRecord contains exactly filePath and text; filePath is one
      normalized workspace-relative path accepted by isSupportedImplementationSource,
      and text is the exact decoded text the host read from that filePath

      complete means every successfully discovered supported source is present,
      including the valid zero-source case; records are ordered by normalized path
      and duplicate normalized paths are rejected

      unavailable requires an empty source collection and at least one discovery
      diagnostic

      discovery diagnostics follow DiagnosticModel ordering and coalescing;
      preserve stage and related locations as part of each complete record
    }

    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      ownedImplementationTargetsFor(resolvedWorkspace, implementationSources,
      componentIdentity, tagName?, sectionName?) returns an owned-implementation
      projection or undefined

      implementationSources is exactly the ordered immutable source collection from
      ImplementationEvidenceInput.sources, not the evidence envelope itself; envelope
      validation and snapshot/discovery-state handling remain with the caller.

      componentIdentity contains the exact case-sensitive componentName and
      normalized workspace-relative declarationPath of one resolved component

      returns undefined only when componentIdentity identifies no exact resolved
      component or tagName is supplied and identifies no Tag in that component

      returns a projection with an empty targets collection when the component and
      optional Tag resolve but no annotation matches the optional section scope

      The query scans implementation comments that link entrypoint definitions or
      agent-facing Markdown back to the selected Sigil component and optional Tag and
      section scopes.
    }

    OwnershipDiagnostics {
      ownershipDiagnosticsFor(resolvedWorkspace, implementationSources) returns all
      deterministic ownership-annotation diagnostics from supported implementation
      sources without requiring a selected component.

      The result includes malformed, detached, unknown-target, and unresolved-section
      diagnostics and excludes valid annotations and unsupported source paths.
    }

    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      includes the owning contract, optional Tag and section scopes, normalized
      implementation targets, and diagnostics
    }

    OwnedImplementationTarget {
      each target identifies one owned implementation artifact

      each target preserves the artifact kind, file path, selected sections, and
      optional symbol identity when available

      location is the one-way entrypoint location for a code annotation or the
      document-start location for agent-facing Markdown. It has no end position and
      is absent only for file-scoped annotations without an entrypoint.

      annotationRange is the exact ownership-comment range used as provenance. It
      remains internal discovery evidence and does not make an annotation a promise
      that the linked implementation completely owns, implements, or remains aligned
      with the selected Sigil sections.
    }

    ImplementationSourceSupport {
      isSupportedImplementationSource(filePath) returns whether the path can contain
      a supported ownership annotation

      supportedImplementationSourceGlobPatterns returns immutable workspace watcher
      patterns covering the same supported source extensions

      supported paths include single-language code, markup, stylesheet, and
      single-file component sources
    }

    ComponentFileRegions {
      A single-file component or markup source is scanned as an ordered set of
      regions rather than as one language.

      Each region carries the comment syntax used to find annotations and the binding
      used to resolve their targets.

      A script region uses the code comment syntax of its enclosing family, a style
      region uses stylesheet block and line comments, and every remaining region is
      template markup.

      A template markup region accepts an HTML comment, and a server-template family
      additionally accepts its own template comment form.

      Binding is exactly symbol, optional-symbol, or file; symbol requires an
      adjacent entrypoint definition, file never resolves one, and optional-symbol
      resolves an adjacent entrypoint when one exists and otherwise binds the
      containing file.
    }

    ImplementationAnnotation {
      identifies a relation, repository-relative Sigil path, component name, optional
      Tag name, and one or more selected sections

      the containing implementation file and adjacent entrypoint definition identify
      the owned implementation target

      uses @sigil <relation> <file>::<component>[::<Tag>] <section>[,<section>...]

      file is a repository-relative Sigil path

      file identifies the source declaring the named component; removed expansion
      locations require the migration locator mapping, not runtime fallback.

      The optional Tag suffix accepts an existing simple unquoted name or one
      JSON-quoted string after the file/component locator. Parse a quoted suffix
      atomically, preserving spaces, quotes, backslashes, and embedded :: exactly.

      implements links an artifact that implements the selected section occurrences.

      uses links an artifact that uses the selected section occurrences.

      tests links an artifact that tests the selected section occurrences.
    }
  }

  logic {
    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      scan supplied implementation sources for supported ownership comments

      resolve each annotation target against the selected Sigil workspace

      resolve each annotation's selected sections against the targeted component or
      Tag occurrences

      normalize code, tests, and agent-facing workflow Markdown into one shared
      result shape

      derive symbol-level targets from annotated language entrypoint definitions and
      use file-level targets for Markdown, template markup, and stylesheets

      preserve the exact entrypoint location and annotationRange in every normalized
      target

      return undefined before scanning when the component or supplied Tag does not
      resolve; otherwise return one projection after scanning even when it has no
      targets
    }

    ComponentFileRegions {
      select the region set from the source extension; a single-language source
      yields one region covering the whole text

      derive embedded script and style regions of a single-file component from its
      opening and closing tags, and treat every uncovered span as template markup

      scan each region with its own comment syntax and shift the resulting comment
      offsets back into whole-source coordinates

      order the combined comments by source offset so targets remain deterministic

      skip an opening tag that falls inside an already selected region
    }

    OwnershipDiagnostics {
      scan every supported implementation source once and collect diagnostics from
      malformed or unresolved ownership annotations without selecting targets.

      order diagnostics according to DiagnosticModel and coalesce only identical
      complete records, retaining distinct stage and related-location evidence.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ImplementationEvidenceInput {
      Ownership lookup preserves supplied source text exactly and performs no
      filesystem discovery or reads.

      Complete and unavailable describe source-enumeration availability, not whether
      any ownership annotation exists.
    }

    AnnotationStorage {
      Ownership annotations exist only in implementation comments and never in Sigil
      source.

      Each annotation points from an implementation artifact to a repository-relative
      Sigil path, component, optional Tag, and selected sections.
    }

    SectionSelection {
      Every annotation selects one or more interface, state, logic, constraints, or
      cases sections.

      Goal and decisions sections cannot be ownership targets.

      For a component target, each selector resolves to that component own Facets in
      the selected existing section.

      For a Tag target, resolve the exact accessible Tag in the named component
      scope. Select only that component own Facets associated with the Tag through
      local grouping, inline introduction, or bare reference in the named sections.
      Keep the originating Tag owner separate; an imported Tag never creates a local
      grouping.

      Empty, repeated, unknown, or unresolved section selectors produce diagnostics
      instead of an inferred relationship.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      A symbol-bound annotation is immediately associated with a language entrypoint
      definition such as a class, function, method, interface, struct, or equivalent
      stable definition.

      One annotation uses the language's single-line comment form.

      Multiple annotations use the language's multiline comment form.

      The comment-form rule applies only where a region offers both a line and a
      multiline comment form; file-bound regions waive it.

      Markdown and template markup annotations use HTML comments.

      Stylesheet annotations use block comments, and Sass or Less may also use line
      comments.

      JSON annotations are outside the current scope.
    }

    ComponentFileRegions {
      Region boundaries are derived from the source text alone and never from a
      framework toolchain, parser plugin, or network resource.

      An embedded script or style block that is not closed extends to the end of the
      source.

      Astro frontmatter delimited by leading triple-dash fences is a script region.

      A template markup or stylesheet region binds every annotation to its file
      because it exposes no stable entrypoint definition.

      A markup source is split on the same terms as a single-file component, so an
      annotation inside an embedded script or style block is resolved rather than
      ignored.

      A supported extension is selected from a template or component family observed
      in real repositories, not from one fixed list.

      A template comment form is recognized only in the server-template family that
      defines it; the same text in another markup or component source is ordinary
      content.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      v1 includes source code and tests with supported comment syntax.

      v1 includes markup, stylesheet, and single-file component sources so a
      presentation surface can carry ownership.

      v1 includes Markdown only for agent-facing instructions or workflow documents.

      Sigil sources, JSON, general prose documentation, configs, and non-text
      artifacts remain out of scope.

      The exported support predicate and watcher patterns describe one shared
      implementation-source extension policy.

      Every watcher-pattern match is accepted by isSupportedImplementationSource,
      while the predicate remains authoritative for received paths.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Resolve source annotations to their adjacent entrypoint definition.

      Resolve agent-facing Markdown annotations at file level.

      Require the annotation Sigil path to declare the exact named component; no
      former expand-only source redirects ownership.

      An imported reference never transfers a consumer implementation claim or
      consumer-owned Facet to the provider.

      Ignore ambiguous or detached annotations and return diagnostics rather than
      inferring ownership.
    }

    LookupAbsenceSemantics {
      An unresolved exact component identity or supplied Tag returns undefined.

      Name-only fallback is forbidden; duplicate component names remain
      distinguishable by declarationPath.

      A resolved lookup with zero matching targets returns an empty projection and
      preserves its owning component, optional Tag, optional section scope, and
      diagnostics.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    SnapshotBoundImplementationEvidence {
      Decision: Accept implementation evidence only through one immutable envelope
      carrying the governing workspace snapshot identity, discovery state, ordered
      source records, and ordered diagnostics.

      Scope: Governs host-to-core implementation evidence supplied to ownership and
      purpose retrieval; excludes filesystem discovery policy and retrieval result
      membership.

      Assumptions: The host copies workspaceSnapshotIdentity from the resolved
      workspace before enumerating implementation sources for that request.

      Trade-offs: Hosts must preserve an explicit envelope and distinguish complete
      zero-source discovery from unavailable discovery, while core lookup remains
      filesystem-free and reproducible.

      Design issues addressed: A bare source array cannot distinguish valid absence
      from failed discovery or prove association with the selected Sigil snapshot.

      Discarded alternatives: Implicit host state, filesystem reads during lookup,
      nullable source arrays, and unbound discovery diagnostics were rejected as
      ambiguous or nondeterministic.

      Consequences: Retrieval can reject cross-snapshot evidence before scanning and
      can preserve complete versus unavailable discovery semantics.

      Revisit when: Core owns an atomic workspace-and-implementation snapshot
      boundary.
    }

    AnnotationStorage {
      Decision: Store ownership links in implementation comments that point back to
      governing Sigil components and Tags.

      Scope: Governs shared CLI and LSP ownership discovery for source code, tests,
      and agent-facing Markdown; excludes annotations inside Sigil and JSON support.

      Trade-offs: Implementation-local links are visible where code changes occur,
      but scanners must understand language comment and entrypoint syntax.

      Design issues addressed: Sigil contracts should remain
      implementation-independent while code retains a reviewable link to its
      governing rationale.

      Consequences: Ownership discovery scans implementation artifacts, and Sigil
      files contain no implementation-link annotations.

      Revisit when: A language cannot expose a stable comment or entrypoint
      association.
    }

    AnnotationGrammar {
      Decision: Encode each ownership link as @sigil <relation>
      <file>::<component>[::<Tag>] <section>[,<section>...] and allow only
      implements, uses, or tests.

      Scope: Governs payloads parsed from supported implementation comments; file is
      a repository-relative Sigil path, component is required, Tag is optional, and
      one or more sections are required.

      Trade-offs: A small fixed vocabulary and required occurrence selectors keep
      ownership queries precise and deterministic, but existing sectionless
      annotations require reviewed migration.

      Design issues addressed: Component-level and Tag-level links otherwise cannot
      identify which contract occurrences the implementation uses, implements, or
      tests.

      Discarded alternatives: follows, validates, related, goal and decisions
      selectors, and sectionless annotations do not provide the selected
      implementation-occurrence precision required by the ownership query.

      Consequences: Parsers reject unsupported relations and annotations without
      valid implementation-related section selectors.

      Revisit when: Ownership queries need another behavioral relation or section
      occurrences are insufficiently precise.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      Decision: Attach annotations to stable language entrypoint definitions, using
      single-line comments for one link and multiline comments for multiple links.

      Scope: Governs source annotation placement and comment form; Markdown uses HTML
      comments and remains file-scoped.

      Trade-offs: Entrypoint placement improves symbol navigation but requires
      language-aware scanning.

      Consequences: Classes, functions, methods, interfaces, structs, and equivalent
      definitions become linkable ownership entrypoints.

      Revisit when: Supported languages require declaration metadata instead of
      comments.
    }

    ScopeSelection {
      Decision: Allow optional Tag and section scopes so a contract can return all
      owned targets, one Tag's targets, or only targets selecting one section
      occurrence.

      Scope: Governs query scoping within one contract; section scope filters
      selected annotation sections and excludes cross-contract aggregation and
      transitive ownership inference.

      Trade-offs: Tag and section scoping increase precision without forcing separate
      query shapes, but callers must supply the occurrence scope when they need a
      narrower view.

      Consequences: The same API serves whole-contract, Tag-specific, and
      section-specific ownership views.

      Revisit when: Component, Tag, and section scoping prove too coarse or too fine
      for routine use.
    }

    SectionSelection {
      Decision: Resolve component selectors against the named component own sections
      and Tag selectors against its own Facets associated through local grouping,
      inline introduction, or bare reference in those sections.

      Scope: Governs annotation validation, projection metadata, and optional
      section-scoped lookup; excludes goal, decisions, and semantic-unit-level
      selectors.

      Trade-offs: Occurrence validation detects stale or impossible links, but moving
      a Tag between sections requires its annotations to be reconciled.

      Design issues addressed: A section name has different precision when the
      annotation targets an entire component versus one Tag.

      Consequences: Both selectors preserve the named component as consumer owner.
      Provider Tag origin is separate and never moves an implementation claim
      upstream.

      Revisit when: Ownership needs semantic-unit-level or cross-component occurrence
      selection.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      Decision: Limit v1 implementation targets to source code and tests with
      supported comments plus agent-facing instruction or workflow Markdown, and
      expose one shared source-support policy for host filtering and watcher
      registration.

      Scope: Governs eligible implementation artifacts, the support predicate, and
      extension-based watcher patterns; excludes filesystem traversal and
      host-specific watcher lifecycle.

      Trade-offs: Shared patterns prevent host integrations from duplicating the
      extension list, while received paths still require predicate validation.

      Consequences: CLI, LSP, and editor integrations can share one definition of
      supported ownership sources.

      Revisit when: Other artifact classes become meaningful implementation evidence
      or a supported host cannot express the shared patterns.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Decision: Resolve source annotations to adjacent language entrypoint
      definitions and resolve agent-facing Markdown at file level.

      Scope: Governs how owned implementation targets are normalized inside the query
      result; excludes detached-annotation inference and human approval.

      Trade-offs: Language-aware entrypoint resolution improves navigation, while
      Markdown remains useful through file-level links.

      Consequences: The same query can link source and test entrypoints plus
      file-level Markdown targets without guessing ambiguous ownership.

      Revisit when: Supported languages need declaration metadata or Markdown
      requires section-level targets.
    }

    LookupAbsenceSemantics {
      Decision: Require exact component name and declaration-path identity, reserve
      undefined for an unresolved exact component or supplied Tag, and return an
      empty projection for every resolved lookup with zero matching targets.

      Scope: Governs OwnedImplementationLookup result presence; excludes annotation
      diagnostics, source discovery, and host failure.

      Trade-offs: Callers must inspect targets and diagnostics separately, but can
      distinguish an invalid contract scope from valid absence of implementation
      ownership evidence.

      Discarded alternatives: Returning undefined for zero matches and returning an
      empty projection for unknown contract identities were rejected because both
      erase a material lookup distinction.

      Consequences: Retrieval and host consumers can treat empty ownership evidence
      as complete while handling unresolved identities explicitly.
    }

    OwnershipDiagnostics {
      Decision: Provide a workspace-wide deterministic ownership-diagnostic scan
      separately from component-scoped target projections.

      Scope: Governs annotation validation without target selection; excludes
      implementation-source discovery, host presentation, and evaluator policy.

      Trade-offs: A separate scan validates annotations for components not selected
      by a host, while callers perform one additional pass over supported sources.

      Consequences: CLI check can expose malformed ownership links without
      duplicating component projection logic or misattributing diagnostics to one
      component.

      Revisit when: Ownership diagnostics need source discovery provenance or
      component-scoped filtering.
    }

    ComponentFileRegions {
      Decision: Extend supported implementation sources to markup, stylesheet, and
      single-file component extensions, and resolve each single-file component as
      per-region comment syntax plus per-region binding.

      Scope: Governs source-support policy, comment scanning, and target binding for
      presentation surfaces; excludes framework-specific parsing, artifact-kind
      changes, and template expression semantics.

      Assumptions: A presentation surface is authored as one file per component, so
      the file is a meaningful ownership target when no exported definition exists.

      Trade-offs: Text-derived regions keep scanning deterministic and dependency
      free, but cannot resolve a symbol inside a template expression and may mis-scan
      a tag written inside a string literal.

      Design issues addressed: A frontend boundary was structurally unrepresentable.
      A Vue, Svelte, or Astro component and its stylesheets could carry no ownership
      annotation, so implementation coverage maps and context summaries silently
      omitted the presentation half of a codebase and made Sigil appear to govern
      backend code only.

      Discarded alternatives: Requiring a symbol entrypoint everywhere was rejected
      because a script-setup component exposes no such definition, which leaves the
      most common idiom unannotatable. Treating a single-file component as one
      language was rejected because its comment syntax differs per region. Treating a
      markup source as one region was rejected because it silently drops an
      annotation placed in an embedded script or style block. Selecting extensions
      from a single fixed list was rejected because a server-rendered template family
      such as Go templates would leave a repository's whole template layer
      unreachable. Adding a distinct presentation artifact kind was rejected as a
      wider public contract change than the coverage gap requires.

      Consequences: A presentation surface participates in ownership projections,
      coverage maps, and watcher registration on the same terms as backend code, and
      the comment-form rule applies only to regions offering both comment forms.

      Consequences: Supporting a server-template comment form keeps an annotation out
      of the rendered page, which an HTML comment in a shipped template cannot do.

      Revisit when: Symbol-level targets are needed inside template markup or
      stylesheet selectors, or a supported framework stops being recoverable from
      source text alone.
    }
  }

  cases {

    FacetTagAssociation {
      A Facet under a local grouping matches that Tag selector even when its prose
      does not repeat the name. An inline-only Tag and an imported Tag referenced in
      consumer prose also support exact selectors without inventing grouping
      declarations. Each resulting link retains the consumer owner, selected
      sections, exact Tag scope, and separate origin.
    }

    QuotedTagSelectors {
      A JSON-quoted Tag suffix containing spaces, an escaped quote, a backslash, or
      :: resolves its exact name atomically. Existing simple unquoted names and
      implements, uses, and tests relations remain valid.

      A Tag with no consumer-owned Facet in the selected section yields the existing
      unresolved-section diagnostic, even when the provider has a matching section.
    }
    ImplementationEvidenceInput {
      Complete discovery with zero records is valid evidence and differs from
      unavailable discovery with zero records and at least one diagnostic.

      Two records that normalize to the same workspace-relative path are rejected
      before ownership scanning.
    }

    ComponentFileRegions {
      A single-file component contributes one target per annotated region, so a
      template comment and a scoped style comment bind to the file while a script
      comment above a function binds to that function.

      An annotation inside a script-setup block with no following definition binds to
      the file instead of producing a detached-annotation diagnostic.

      One annotation inside a stylesheet block comment is valid because plain CSS
      offers no line-comment form.

      A scheme separator such as the one in url(https://example.test/a.png) does not
      begin a stylesheet line comment.

      Astro frontmatter between leading triple-dash fences resolves a symbol-level
      target from the definition following its annotation.

      An annotation inside an embedded script block of a markup source resolves to
      the definition that follows it rather than being silently dropped.

      A server-template comment carries an annotation without emitting it to the
      rendered page, while an HTML comment in the same source remains valid.

      A server-template comment form written in an HTML or single-file component
      source is ordinary content and yields neither a target nor a diagnostic.
    }

    ImplementationSourceSupport {
      A supported source path is accepted by the predicate and matched by a shared
      watcher pattern.

      A single-file component, template markup, and stylesheet path are supported
      sources.

      Sigil, JSON, unsupported, and extensionless paths are rejected by the
      predicate.
    }

    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      A TypeScript line comment immediately before one function links that function
      to a Sigil component or Tag.

      A language multiline comment contains multiple ownership annotations for one
      class, method, interface, struct, or equivalent entrypoint.

      An HTML comment links an agent-facing Markdown workflow file to a Sigil
      component or Tag.

      A component annotation can implement logic and constraints occurrences without
      naming a Tag.

      A Tag annotation can use the interface occurrence of that Tag.

      JSON content is ignored.

      An unknown exact component identity or supplied Tag returns undefined.

      Two components with the same name but different declaration paths are queried
      independently and never share ownership targets through name-only fallback.

      A resolved exact component with no matching annotation returns an empty
      projection.
    }

    OwnershipDiagnostics {
      A workspace check can report an invalid ownership annotation even when its
      target component is not otherwise selected by a host command.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      A source annotation adjacent to a supported entrypoint produces a symbol-level
      implementation target.

      A Markdown instruction comment produces a file-level implementation target.

      A detached or ambiguous annotation produces a diagnostic instead of an inferred
      target.

      A Tag annotation naming a section where the Tag does not occur produces a
      diagnostic.

      A consumer annotation selecting an imported Tag points to consumer-owned Facets
      in selected sections while retaining the separate provider Tag identity.
    }
  }
}
